{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3ad","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b119-7781-7bc0-8528-77290b455925"},"sequence":49,"recorded_at":1780531400000093,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"13d59c78-6f33-4ca9-9b77-41caa4df923d","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"13d59c78-6f33-4ca9-9b77-41caa4df923d","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"13d59c78-6f33-4ca9-9b77-41caa4df923d"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b119-a0ed-7ae3-9635-4c2aa6d1d776"},"task_id":"01a0b119-a0ed-7ae3-9635-4c2aa6d1d776","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b119-a0ed-7ae3-9635-4c2aa6d1d776","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-19bjfj/TASK-260830-19bjfj_producer.md`.\n1|Own TASK-260830-19bjfj (fuzz-rpc-operations-and-version-skew), the FINAL leaf of STORY-260830-4qojoz (mesh-rpc-framing-and-negotiation, EPIC M2). Predecessor TASK-260830-219okr (dual-stack major negotiation, internal/meshneg: LocalMajors/PeerOffer/Select/Negotiate with its Decision/PeerView/Refusal/FeatureActivation types) is accepted and checkpointed on the Story branch. Your Change Request is the story_final whole-Story patch, so read the whole branch delta first. This leaf is the adversarial closure of Sections 11.2-11.3 and 17 over the landed owners internal/rpcwire (envelope, framing, majors 2-5, the existing FuzzUntrustedEnvelopes gate) , internal/hostchannel (RPC 5 endpoint admission) and internal/meshneg — extend them, never fork a second envelope, namespace or negotiation model.\n2|Scope:\n3|(1) Closed operation bodies. Section 11.3 lists twenty-four operations (hello, health.get, inventory.roots, inventory.children, objects.get, transfer.begin, transfer.status, chunks.put, transfer.validate, transfer.commit, materialize.prepare, materialize.commit, materialize.status, materialize.finalize, materialize.rollback, lease.refresh, tombstone.ack, session.status, session.stop, handoff.prepare, handoff.quiesce, handoff.stop, handoff.commit, handoff.abort). For EVERY operation whose body the landed code parses, drive the closed-shape contract through the production entry: exact member set (a missing member, an extra top-level member and a member of the wrong type each refuse with the pinned class), every bound witnessed at both edges and one step outside, every embedded closed type (Namespace record|event|manifest|tombstone|tombstone_ack|blob, LeaseExpectation, LeaseHead, GroupMemberExpectation, WorkspaceGroupExpectation, MaterializationKind, MaterializationIntent) admitted only by its exact vocabulary, `initiator_host_id` present on every mutation body and an exact lease expectation on every session mutation, both revalidated immediately before the mutation. Any operation the landed code does not yet parse is a STATED BOUND, listed by name in the results and the conformance matrix — do not implement new operations to make a row green, and do not claim a row you did not drive.\n4|(2) Fuzzing. Add bounded fuzz targets (seeded corpora, `-fuzztime=100x`, `-parallel=1`, deterministic, no network) that reach the body parsers rather than only the envelope: a body fuzzer keyed over the operation vocabulary, a namespace fuzzer, and an unknown-field fuzzer. Every target must have a control: plant a body the target is supposed to reject, prove the target fails on it, then remove the plant — a fuzz target that cannot fail is not evidence. Register the new targets in task-board.config.json as bounded validation commands in the same shape as the existing `go test ./internal/rpcwire -run=^$ -fuzz=^FuzzUntrustedEnvelopes$ -fuzztime=100x -parallel=1` row, say so explicitly in the results, and note that Change Request construction runs the CONTROL ROOT's suite, so you must run the new commands yourself and attach their raw logs.\n5|(3) Section 17 compatibility, driven not asserted: within a negotiated major, new object data lives under a namespaced `extensions` entry and an unknown TOP-LEVEL field remains an error (prove both directions: a namespaced extension is admitted, the same data at top level refuses); peers choose the highest mutually supported minor within a common major and MUST NOT select a major by coercion (a peer offering only a higher major, only a lower major, an empty set, a duplicate, an unsorted array and an out-of-range count each produce the pinned refusal through internal/meshneg, not a coerced selection); a 2.0.0 peer rejects major 1 rather than reinterpreting it; the embedded Structured Error version is fixed by the containing protocol and is never negotiated separately (RPC 2 binds Error 1.0.0, RPC 4/5 bind Error 1.3.0) — drive a peer that tries to negotiate the error schema and prove it refuses.\n6|(4) Replay and lost response. Every request carries a UUIDv7 `request_id` and every response echoes it: prove a mismatched echo refuses, a duplicate `request_id` with an identical body replays the same result, and a duplicate `request_id` with a CHANGED body is `idempotency_mismatch`. For the lost-response path drive `materialize.status` as the recovery entry (it exists to recover the exact durable phase after a lost response or restart) and prove that a client that lost the result learns the true phase instead of guessing, and that a blind retry after a committed effect never doubles it.\n7|(5) Framing. Line-delimited JSON with a maximum line of 8 MiB: witness 8388608 accepted and 8388609 refused, an embedded newline, a truncated line, a line that is valid JSON but not an object, unpadded base64url chunk bytes accepted and padded/standard-alphabet bytes refused, and `max_object_bytes` enforced independently of `max_line_bytes`.\n8|Tests and evidence: every row driven through a production entry with a named committed test, an n-of-m ratio and call sites; a narrowing mutant per gate (drop a member check, widen a bound by one, accept an unsorted array, skip the pre-mutation revalidation, let a major be coerced) executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control; and a conformance matrix over every 11.2/11.3/17.1-17.4 clause mapped to a test, a stated bound, or NOT APPLICABLE with its owner.\n9|COMMON CONTRACT (FINAL leaf of its Story — your Change Request is the story_final whole-Story patch). Normative authority: the pinned internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md headings named above; the v0.7.0 adoption (STORY-260917-110onn) may land on trunk during your run — if it has landed when you refresh, cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers and use the v0.7.0 artifacts everywhere the v0.6.0 ones are named below. Reuse the landed owners (canonicaljson closed shapes, scalar, sessrepo/sessstate/sessquery, sessckpt, matjournal, crashgate, fencing, sessprofile, provhost/resumesmoke, hosttrust/peeridentity/sshtransport, rpcwire/hostchannel, sessadapter, terminalbackend, localstore, secprim/secconftest) and above all the packages your own Story's earlier leaves added on this branch — never fork a second model of anything already modeled; extend or adopt and say which. STORY-CLOSE ITEMS, all owned by this leaf: (a) internal/traceability ownership registry bindings for every normative section this Story claims plus the Story's acceptance case bound to the REAL conformance suite with honest coverage text that separates executed lanes from stated bounds (precedent Stories 3drr2m/3jqsx1/3m2mw8/2jylym/1kiyj6) — edit the ADOPTED registry, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json` if the v0.7.0 adoption is on trunk when you refresh, otherwise `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.6.0.json`, and never invent a binding for a clause no test drives; (b) re-derive and re-pin `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256` in internal/traceability/traceability.go from the semantic JSON projection of the registry you just edited — derive it, never copy a digest from another Story, and show the derivation command and output in the results; (c) `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` green plus the section-scoped run for each section you bound, with the measured coverage ratio quoted verbatim in the results; (d) README: a section for each package this Story added with its exact test commands and NO capability or CLI claim (no `ax` command exists), and the \"Measured coverage of this repository\" subsection updated to the numbers tracecheck actually prints; (e) one newest-first LOGBOOK entry for the Story. The candidate's task-board.config.json must stay byte-identical to trunk unless you deliberately add a bounded validation command — if you add one, say so explicitly in the results and keep it fast. Evidence contract (repository DoD): every AC row driven through a production entry by a named committed test with a stated n-of-m ratio and call sites; negatives for every refusal; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control (PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__); crash/idempotency evidence for every durable write or an explicit purity bound; a conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test, a stated bound or NOT APPLICABLE with an owner. Managed Story worktree contract: the Story worktree .temp/STORY-260830-4qojoz/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-4qojoz ALREADY EXISTS and its tip is the previous leaf's signed checkpoint commit — do not re-provision, rebase, commit on, amend or revert it, never edit .task-board directly, never self-accept. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED working tree on top of that checkpoint; the CR the runtime builds from it is the WHOLE-Story patch, so read every earlier leaf's delta (`git -C .temp/STORY-260830-4qojoz/worktree log --stat <trunk>..HEAD`) and its outcome resources before you start. If origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-19bjfj` (LOGBOOK append conflicts are resolved with --replay-resolutions: copy the retained resolution-template.json header and add {\"checkpoint_oid\": <the ORIGINAL checkpoint OID recorded before the refresh>, \"path\": \"LOGBOOK.md\", \"sha256\": <hex of the full replacement>, \"content\": <base64>} keeping every entry newest-first), then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that also appears in your `git status` and restore trunk content by hand — a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies of trunk-changed files into the candidate and they read as reverts (README/LOGBOOK purely additive; task-board.config.json == HEAD; the ownership registry must MERGE the trunk rows with yours, never replace them). Before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first; all CI local; run the full configured 27-command validation suite once before handoff (command 5 is `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m` and can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs — that is expected, do not shorten it; command 21 is `cataloggen -adopted … -check`). Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in — if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-19bjfj_results.md, TASK-260830-19bjfj_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-19bjfj_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-19bjfj --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\n10|\n11|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n12|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n13|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n14|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n15|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n16|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n17|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n18|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\n19|\n20|INHERITED ADVISORIES from the independent review of the predecessor leaf (RUN-260917-b1ebcb accepted TASK-260830-219okr CR rev4 and recorded three P3 items explicitly \"for the next producer on this Story\" — that is you). None is a production defect; each is an unmeasured gate in the code you are extending, so fix it as part of your own evidence work and say so in your results:\n21|- N1: the `PeerOffer` key-membership gate (negotiate.go:279-283) is pinned at one hand-picked key. The committed negative substitutes only `lease`, and the producer's own plant aims at that same witnessed member; a per-key sweep of 25 plants killed 1 (`rpc` is subsumed by the rpc-length arm, so the ratio for the membership gate proper is 1 of 24 non-rpc keys). Derive the substituted-key and missing-key cases from `rpcwire.ContractProfile(frame)` for EVERY frame and EVERY key, so any per-key exemption reddens. This is the same shape as the repo memory \"duplicate-member gate pinned per key\" — a narrowing aimed at the witnessed member measures nothing.\n22|- N2: `Refusal.Local` on offer-originated refusals is unpinned through `Negotiate` (negotiate.go:490-493 copies the local offer into a PeerOffer refusal and the doc makes it a reported fact, but a plant setting `refusal.Local = nil` survived the committed suite twice). Assert `refusal.Local == LocalMajors(generation)` in one shape case and one mixed case, both driven through `Negotiate`.\n23|- N3: the `Select` local-side vocabulary refuses majors outside 2/3/4/5, but the refused table carries 6, -2 and 0 and not `1`, so a `major < 1` plant survived twice. It is unreachable through `Negotiate` (LocalMajors never yields 1) and only a direct `Select` caller sees it — add the one `refuse-local-names-1` row and state the reachability bound.","final_result":true}}}
